<!--
    GH-9203: a late conditional-branch simplification inserts a MULTIEQUAL input
    p-code op into the wrong (loop) block, manifesting as a malformed do/while
    whose body holds a spurious constant assignment.
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/9203
    Ported from kuna's gh9203-condexe-loopcopy stage testcase (GayHydra #477).

    ActionConditionalConst (coreaction.cc) propagates a value known-constant along
    a conditional out-edge.  When the value reaches a MULTIEQUAL on a disconnected
    incoming edge, handlePhiNodes() materializes the constant as a fresh COPY op
    placed at the BOTTOM of that edge's predecessor block and rewires the MULTIEQUAL
    input to it.  Here the predecessor is the bottom of a LOOP, so the (tmp = 0) COPY
    lands inside the loop body and is re-executed every iteration:

      iVar1 = 7L;
      do {
        iVar1 = iVar1 - 1L;
        xVar2 = 0L;            out-of-place: belongs outside the loop
      } while (iVar1 != 0L);

    "option condexeplace on|off" (default off = stock behaviour) makes
    handlePhiNodes() decline the placement when the MULTIEQUAL edge's predecessor
    block has a looping in-edge (bl->hasLoopIn()) and leave the MULTIEQUAL input
    intact, so the constant is assigned outside the loop body.

    Pass 1 (default pipeline, option off) reproduces the bug: the do/while body
    carries the out-of-place (xVar2 = 0L;).
    Pass 2 (after option condexeplace on) shows the loop body no longer carries
    a bare "= 0L;" assignment.

    Assert #1 keys on "= 0L;" with min=max=1: it must appear exactly once across
    both passes (present in pass 1, gone in pass 2), proving both the bug and the
    fix at once.  Assert #2 keys on "do {" with min=max=2: the loop structure
    itself is preserved in both passes (we did not destroy control flow).

    kuna's original script also pins "option returndup off"; the fork has no
    returndup transform (the shared epilogue is never split), so that line is dropped.
-->
